import CourseLayout from "../../layouts/CourseLayout.astro";
import WeekSchedule from "../../components/WeekSchedule.astro";
import {withBase} from "astro-theme-university/url";
---
<CourseLayout title="Enter the training ground." description="Twelve interactive labs, each with a complete browser experience and a campus equivalent." eyebrow="THE COURSE / LABS" wide>
<section class="callout"><h2>Your first lab, step by step</h2><ol><li><strong>Open a week and read its mission.</strong> It names the skill, exact actions and what you will produce. Use the briefing or separate demonstration if the idea is unfamiliar.</li><li><strong>Play Practice.</strong> Use the named equipment controls, make a prediction and check the result. Camera movement helps you see the apparatus; it is not the learning task.</li><li><strong>Explain and save the attempt afterwards.</strong> Then try Skill check and Transfer challenge, which change the conditions. Save each phase you want to keep before switching.</li><li><strong>Keep your evidence.</strong> Export a backup and assemble the short fieldwork record. Your best ten of twelve weekly records count.</li></ol><div class="actions"><a class="button primary" href={withBase("/sessions/week-01/")}>Start Lab 1 ↗</a><a class="button" href={withBase("/assessments/fieldwork/")}>What goes in a fieldwork record?</a></div></section>
<p>The six rows in each two-hour timetable are teaching stages: Briefing, Learn, Practise, Test, Transfer and Debrief. The three playable configurations are <strong>Practice</strong>, <strong>Skill check</strong> and <strong>Transfer challenge</strong>. There is no time limit or hidden objective to unlock.</p>
<h2>Choose a lab</h2>
<WeekSchedule/>
</CourseLayout>
